---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Chainsaws",
    "url": "/best-chainsaw/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Chainsaw Sharpeners",
    "url": "/best-chainsaw-sharpener/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Hedge Trimmers",
    "url": "/best-hedge-trimmer/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Log Splitters",
    "url": "/best-log-splitter/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Hookaroons",
    "url": "/best-hookaroon-reviews/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Telescoping Ladders",
    "url": "/best-telescoping-ladder/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-10-06", modified: "2026-10-06" });
const title = "7 Best Pole Saws in 2026: Battery, Manual & Gas Compared";
const description =
  "Seven pole saws compared on reach, bar length, weight and power, from brushless battery saws to 21-foot manual saws, plus the power line rules you must follow.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "dewalt-dcps620b",
    "name": "DeWalt 20V MAX XR Brushless Pole Saw, Tool Only (DCPS620B)",
    "category": "8\" Battery, 20V MAX Brushless",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.6,
    "reviewCount": 8000,
    "pros": [
      "Up to 15 feet of total reach with a pole that extends to 10 feet",
      "Brushless motor rated for up to 96 cuts per charge on 4x4 pressure-treated pine with a 4Ah battery",
      "8-inch low-kickback bar and chain",
      "Automatic oiling for the bar and chain",
      "Metal bucking strip and tree hook for gripping and pulling down cut branches"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Sold bare, so it is expensive if you do not already own DeWalt 20V batteries",
      "An 8-inch bar limits it to pruning-sized limbs"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The pole saw most homeowners should buy: enough reach for typical yard trees, real cutting power, and a battery platform millions of people already own.",
    "description": "DeWalt's DCPS620B is the pole saw we would hand to most homeowners, and its huge rating count suggests plenty of them agree. The pole extends up to 10 feet, which DeWalt says gives a total reach of up to 15 feet for a 6-foot person holding the saw at shoulder height at a 60-degree angle. That is a realistic figure rather than a marketing stretch, and it covers the lower and middle canopy of most yard trees. The brushless motor drives an 8-inch low-kickback bar and chain, and DeWalt rates it for up to 96 cuts per charge in 4x4 pressure-treated pine with a 4Ah battery, which is far more than a typical weekend of pruning demands. The small details are the ones that make pole sawing easier. Automatic oiling keeps the bar lubricated while the cutting head is 12 feet above you where you cannot check it, a metal bucking strip bites into the branch so the saw does not skate, and a tree hook lets you pull hung-up branches down without climbing. The limitations are size and cost. An 8-inch bar is for pruning limbs, not felling, and the bare tool only makes financial sense if you already own DeWalt 20V packs. If you do, this is an easy recommendation."
  },
  {
    "id": "ego-ps1000",
    "name": "EGO Power+ 10-Inch Telescopic Carbon Fiber Pole Saw, Tool Only (PS1000)",
    "category": "10\" Battery, 56V Brushless",
    "badge": "Best Premium Battery Pole Saw",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.6,
    "reviewCount": 250,
    "pros": [
      "Telescopic carbon fiber shaft with a limited lifetime warranty",
      "Up to 13 feet of total reach, or up to 17 feet with the EP1000 extension pole",
      "LED cut line indicator for accurate cuts in shaded canopy",
      "10-inch bar with 1/4-inch pitch, 0.043-inch gauge chain at up to 20 m/s",
      "Up to 100 cuts per charge with a 2.5Ah battery, plus tool-free chain tensioning"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Battery and charger sold separately",
      "Base reach is shorter than DeWalt's unless you add the extension pole"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best-engineered battery pole saw we found, worth the premium if you already own EGO batteries or prune often.",
    "description": "EGO's PS1000 is a dedicated pole saw, not an attachment, and it shows in the details. The telescopic shaft is carbon fiber, which EGO backs with a limited lifetime warranty, and it extends for a total reach of up to 13 feet, or up to 17 feet with the optional EP1000 extension pole. Carbon fiber matters because weight at the end of a long shaft tires your shoulders, and a stiffer shaft wobbles less when you are trying to place the chain on a branch 15 feet away. The 10-inch bar is longer than the 8-inch bars on most battery pole saws, and it runs a 1/4-inch pitch, 0.043-inch gauge chain at up to 20 meters per second, driven by a brushless motor. EGO rates it for up to 100 cuts per charge with a 2.5Ah battery. Its unique feature is an LED cut line indicator, which lights the cut point when you are working under a dense, shaded canopy, exactly where it is hardest to see. Chain tensioning is tool-free, and there is a shoulder strap hook. The drawbacks are price and base reach: the battery and charger are extra, and out of the box it reaches a little less far than DeWalt's DCPS620B. For EGO owners, it is the best pole saw available."
  },
  {
    "id": "greenworks-po80-pole-saw",
    "name": "Greenworks 40V 8-Inch Pole Saw and 20-Inch Pole Hedge Trimmer Combo Kit (PSPH40B210)",
    "category": "8\" Battery Combo, 40V",
    "badge": "Best Value Combo",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.3,
    "reviewCount": 3400,
    "pros": [
      "Two tools in one kit: an 8-inch pole saw and a 20-inch pole hedge trimmer",
      "Hedge head pivots through 7 positions for cutting tops and faces",
      "Automatic oiler for the bar and chain",
      "Easy-adjust chain tensioning",
      "Includes a 2.0Ah battery and charger, with a 3-year warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "The 2.0Ah battery limits long sessions without a spare",
      "Not as strong in hardwood as the brushless DeWalt and EGO saws"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best buy for a homeowner who needs both a pole saw and a pole hedge trimmer and does not want to spend twice.",
    "description": "If you have tall hedges and overhanging limbs, Greenworks' 40V combo kit solves both problems for less than the price of many single pole saws. It pairs an 8-inch pole saw with a 20-inch pole hedge trimmer, and the hedge head pivots through seven positions, so you can lay it flat across the top of a hedge or angle it down the face from the ground. The saw head has an automatic oiler to keep the bar and chain lubricated and an easy-adjust tensioning system, so you can tighten a stretched chain without hunting for a scrench. The kit includes a 2.0Ah battery and charger, and Greenworks backs it with a 3-year warranty. The battery runs any of Greenworks' many 40V tools, which makes the combo a sensible way into the platform. Expectations need to be realistic. This is a homeowner pruning tool for small limbs and hedge tops, not a replacement for a full-size chainsaw or a brushless pro pole saw, and the small 2.0Ah pack will not get through a long day without a recharge or a second battery. Greenworks lists the kit at about 11 pounds. For occasional pruning and twice-a-year hedge tops, though, it is the best value here, and having both heads means you will actually use it."
  },
  {
    "id": "sun-joe-swj802e",
    "name": "Sun Joe 8-Inch 6.5-Amp Electric Pole Chain Saw (SWJ802E)",
    "category": "8\" Corded Electric, 6.5 Amp",
    "badge": "Best Budget Corded",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.5,
    "reviewCount": 3000,
    "pros": [
      "6.5-amp motor rated for branches up to 7.5 inches thick",
      "Telescoping pole extends to 9.4 feet for up to 15 feet of overhead reach",
      "Multi-position head cuts at 0, 15 and 30 degrees",
      "No batteries to buy or charge",
      "CSA approved with a 2-year warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Extension cord limits range and adds drag at full extension",
      "Brushed corded motor is noisier and less refined than battery saws"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The cheapest credible way to prune a few trees near the house, if you do not mind managing a cord.",
    "description": "For homeowners who prune a couple of trees near the house once or twice a year, a corded pole saw is the cheapest route, and Sun Joe's SWJ802E is the one we would buy. The 6.5-amp motor drives an 8-inch bar and chain, and Sun Joe rates it for branches up to 7.5 inches thick, which is more than most people will ever ask of a pole saw. The telescoping pole extends to 9.4 feet, which Sun Joe says provides up to 15 feet of overhead reach, roughly matching DeWalt's battery saw. The head adjusts to 0, 15 and 30 degrees, which helps you get the bar square to branches that grow at awkward angles. Because there is no battery, there is nothing to buy beyond an outdoor extension cord, nothing to charge and no pack to replace in five years. The trade-offs are the cord and the feel. A long cord trailing up a pole adds weight and drag, and you need to keep it clear of the chain and of anything you are dropping. It is also louder and less refined than brushless battery saws. It is CSA approved and carries a 2-year warranty. If you only prune occasionally and have an outlet within reach of your trees, it does the job for a fraction of the price."
  },
  {
    "id": "silky-hayauchi-179-39",
    "name": "Silky Hayauchi 390 21-Foot Telescoping Pole Saw (179-39)",
    "category": "Manual, 15.4\" Blade",
    "badge": "Best for Maximum Reach",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 900,
    "pros": [
      "Four-section pole extends from 8 to 21 feet",
      "Silky quotes a maximum working reach of 26 feet",
      "15.4-inch (390 mm) blade in SK4 high-carbon steel, 5.5 teeth per inch",
      "About 6.9 pounds operating weight",
      "Limited lifetime warranty against defects"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Manual cutting is slow and tiring on limbs over a few inches thick",
      "Premium price for a hand tool"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The arborist favorite: lighter, quieter and longer-reaching than any powered pole saw, for anyone with tall trees and patience.",
    "description": "The Silky Hayauchi is the manual pole saw professional arborists carry, and once you use one it is easy to see why. The four-section aluminum pole extends from 8 feet to 21 feet, and Silky quotes a maximum working reach of 26 feet, far beyond any battery pole saw here. The 15.4-inch blade is made from SK4 high-carbon steel, 1.5 mm thick, with 5.5 teeth per inch, and it cuts on the pull stroke with an aggressiveness that surprises people used to cheap pruning saws. Because there is no motor, the whole tool weighs about 6.9 pounds in operation, and the weight is spread along the pole rather than concentrated at the tip, so it is less tiring to hold aloft than a powered saw. The oval-section poles stop the blade from twisting in your hands, which helps you keep the cut straight. Silky backs it with a limited lifetime warranty against defects. The limitation is effort. On a 2-inch limb, the Hayauchi is quick and clean. On a 6-inch limb at full extension, it is a workout. It is also expensive for a hand tool. But if you have tall trees, want silence and zero maintenance, or need to reach above where a powered pole saw ends, there is nothing better."
  },
  {
    "id": "fiskars-394731-1002",
    "name": "Fiskars 7-16 Ft Chain-Drive Extendable Tree Pruner and Pole Saw (394731-1002)",
    "category": "Manual Pruner and Saw",
    "badge": "Best Budget Manual",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 2100,
    "pros": [
      "Pole adjusts from 7 to 16 feet",
      "Chain-drive pruner multiplies leverage up to 3 times for branches up to 1.25 inches",
      "15-inch WoodZig saw blade with a hooked end for larger limbs",
      "Oval fiberglass and aluminum pole for rigidity at length",
      "Lifetime warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Saw blade is slower than the Silky on thick limbs",
      "Rope-operated pruner takes practice to use well at full extension"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The cheapest way to keep small trees and overhanging branches in check without a motor, and a pruner and saw in one tool.",
    "description": "Fiskars' 7-16 foot extendable pruner is two tools on one pole, and for many homeowners it is the only pole saw they will ever need. The top carries a bypass pruner driven by a chain-drive mechanism that Fiskars says multiplies your cutting power up to three times compared with traditional pruners, for branches up to 1.25 inches thick. That covers most of the routine work on fruit trees, ornamentals and overhanging growth along a driveway. For bigger limbs, a 15-inch WoodZig saw blade with a hooked end anchors against the branch so it does not skate as you cut. The pole adjusts from 7 to 16 feet and is made from oval fiberglass and aluminum, which keeps it light while limiting the flex that makes cheap pole pruners hard to aim. Fiskars' advanced double-locking system, a flip lock plus an internal button lock, stops the sections collapsing mid-cut. The blades are hardened steel with a low-friction coating to resist rust and sap. The saw is not as fast or as aggressive as the Silky, and pulling a rope to work a pruner 16 feet up takes some practice. Fiskars backs it with a lifetime warranty, and it costs a fraction of any powered option."
  },
  {
    "id": "echo-ppt-2620",
    "name": "ECHO 12-Inch 25.4cc Gas Telescoping Power Pruner (PPT-2620)",
    "category": "12\" Gas, 25.4cc 2-Stroke",
    "badge": "Best Gas Pole Saw",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.5,
    "reviewCount": 90,
    "pros": [
      "25.4cc professional-grade 2-stroke engine",
      "12-inch bar, the longest on this list",
      "Telescopes from 107 to 146 inches for up to 16 feet of reach, 20 feet with an optional extension",
      "Automatic, adjustable oiler and side-access chain tensioner",
      "5-year consumer warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "17.4 pounds dry, heavy to hold at full extension",
      "Needs mixed fuel and regular engine maintenance"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The pole saw for acreage and storm cleanup, when you need a 12-inch bar and a tank you can refill in the field.",
    "description": "Battery pole saws handle most suburban pruning, but on larger properties, orchards and after storms, a gas power pruner is still the tool professionals reach for. ECHO's PPT-2620 is our pick. It runs a 25.4cc professional-grade 2-stroke engine and carries a 12-inch bar, the longest on this list, which lets it cut larger limbs in a single pass. The shaft telescopes from 107 inches to 146 inches, and ECHO quotes up to 16 feet of reach, or 20 feet with an optional 4-foot extension. The automatic oiler is adjustable, so you can match oil flow to the wood and the weather, and the magnesium gear case has a side-access chain tensioner, which makes adjustments easier when the saw is lying on the ground. A two-stage air filter extends service intervals, and the 20.6-ounce fuel tank keeps you working for a long time between fill-ups. ECHO backs it with a 5-year consumer warranty. The costs are weight and upkeep. At 17.4 pounds dry, it is heavy to hold at full extension, and like any 2-stroke engine it needs fresh mixed fuel and seasonal maintenance. It also has the smallest Amazon rating count here, so we lean on ECHO's specifications and reputation. For occasional suburban pruning, choose battery."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Pole Saws",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-pole-saw/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best pole saw for homeowners?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most homeowners, a brushless battery pole saw with an 8- or 10-inch bar and around 15 feet of reach is the best fit. The DeWalt DCPS620B is our top pick, and the EGO PS1000 is the premium choice. If you already own a battery platform, buy that brand's pole saw to avoid paying for a new battery and charger."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How high can a pole saw reach?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most battery and corded pole saws quote around 13 to 15 feet of total reach, which includes your own height and arm reach. Gas pruners with extensions can reach about 20 feet, and manual telescoping saws like the Silky Hayauchi extend to 21 feet. Plan to work a couple of feet below the quoted maximum for control."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How thick a branch can a pole saw cut?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Plan on cutting limbs up to about the length of the bar in one pass: 8 inches for most battery saws, 10 to 12 inches for premium and gas models. In practice, most pruning cuts are 2 to 4 inches. Larger limbs should be removed in sections, and anything very large is a job for a tree service."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a manual pole saw better than a powered one?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A manual saw is lighter, quieter, maintenance-free and often reaches higher, which makes it excellent for light pruning and tall trees. A powered saw is much faster and less tiring on limbs over a couple of inches. Many people with mature trees own one of each and choose by the job."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is it safe to use a pole saw near power lines?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Keep yourself, the pole and the branch at least 10 feet from any overhead line, which is OSHA's minimum clearance for unqualified workers near lines up to 50 kilovolts. Consumer pole saws are not insulated, even with fiberglass poles. If branches are near a line, contact your utility; many trim them for free."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use a pole saw from a ladder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "You should not. A pole saw is designed to let you prune with both feet on the ground. Using one from a ladder puts you off-balance with a running saw overhead and no free hand. If you cannot reach a branch from the ground, use a longer saw, leave it, or hire an arborist."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "When is the best time to prune trees with a pole saw?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Late winter, while trees are dormant, is best for most structural pruning, because you can see the branch structure and cuts heal quickly in spring. Dead, damaged or hazardous branches can be removed at any time of year. Avoid removing more than about a quarter of the canopy in one year."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Lawn & Yard Care",
    tags: [
      "best pole saw",
      "battery pole saw",
      "cordless pole saw",
      "manual pole saw",
      "gas pole saw",
      "telescoping pole saw"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            7 Pole Saws Compared
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Pole Saws</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          A pole saw keeps you on the ground and off the ladder. Here is how to pick the right reach and power for your trees, and the safety rules that matter more than either.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#dewalt-dcps620b"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See the Top Picks
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="7.8k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Battery, Corded, Gas or Manual</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#reach" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Reach Explained</a>
          <a href="#bar-length" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Bar Length and Chain</a>
          <a href="#weight" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Weight and Balance</a>
          <a href="#power-lines" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Power Lines and Safety</a>
          <a href="#pruning" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. When and How to Prune</a>
          <a href="#maintenance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Maintenance and Storage</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best pole saw is the one that lets you prune from the ground with both feet planted. For most yards that is an 8- or 10-inch battery saw on a platform you already own; a manual saw reaches higher than any of them, and gas only makes sense on acreage. None of them belong anywhere near a power line.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We looked at the pole saws people actually buy for yard trees: battery saws from the big platforms, a corded saw for small yards, a gas power pruner for larger properties, and two manual saws that professional arborists and orchard owners rely on. We then compared them on reach, bar length, cutting power, weight and how easy they are to control at full extension.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reach is the headline number, but we read it carefully. Manufacturers quote total reach assuming a person of average height holding the saw at shoulder level and an angle, so a 10-foot pole becomes 15 feet of reach on paper. That is fair, but it is also the very top of the range, and you should plan to work a couple of feet below it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every specification here comes from the manufacturer or the retail listing, and ratings are the Amazon figures at the time of writing, rounded down. Prices move constantly, so we describe value in words. We also excluded several older models that are no longer made and only sold from leftover stock.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, we were clear about what a pole saw is not. It is a pruning tool for limbs you can reach from the ground, not a felling saw and not a substitute for a tree service. If the limb is larger than your thigh, hanging over a roof, or anywhere near a power line, the right tool is the phone.
          </p>
        </div>

