          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Battery, Corded, Gas or Manual
          </h2>

          <p>
            Battery pole saws are the right choice for most homeowners. They start instantly, are quiet, need no fuel, and the best brushless models cut quickly through limbs of several inches. The cost is the battery, so the smart buy is a saw on a platform you already own, whether that is DeWalt, EGO, Greenworks or another major brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            Corded pole saws are cheaper and lighter at the head because there is no battery, and they never run out of charge. The trade-off is range and the cord itself, which adds drag at full extension and has to be kept clear of falling branches. They suit small yards with a few trees near an outlet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gas pole saws, often sold as power pruners, are the professional choice for acreage, orchards and storm cleanup. They carry longer bars and run for hours on a tank, but they are heavy, loud and need maintenance. Manual pole saws are the quiet outlier: lighter, longer and maintenance-free, at the cost of your own effort.
          </p>

