        <div id="reach" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Reach: How High You Can Really Cut
          </h2>

          <p>
            Total reach is the pole length plus your own height and arm reach, adjusted for the angle at which you hold the saw. DeWalt, for example, quotes 15 feet of reach for its 10-foot pole, assuming a 6-foot person holding the saw at shoulder height at 60 degrees. If you are shorter, or the ground is sloped, you will reach less.
          </p>

          <p>
            Working at a steep angle is also more tiring and less accurate than working at a moderate one, because the weight of the head sits further from your hands. Most people cut best with the saw between about 45 and 60 degrees, standing well to the side of the branch rather than beneath it. That means planning cuts so you can reach them from a safe position.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you need more than about 15 feet, look at saws that accept an extension pole, like the EGO PS1000 with its optional EP1000 or the ECHO PPT-2620 with its 4-foot extension. Or consider a manual saw: the Silky Hayauchi extends to 21 feet and is light enough to use at that length. Beyond that, you are in tree service territory.
          </p>

          <p>
            Resist the urge to gain reach by standing on something. Pole saws exist so you can keep both feet on the ground. If a branch is just out of reach, it is better to leave it for next year, call a professional, or use a longer manual saw than to work from a ladder. We explain why in the safety section below, and our guide to the <a href="/best-telescoping-ladder/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best telescoping ladders</a> covers the jobs where a ladder is the right answer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bar-length" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Bar Length, Chain and Cutting Capacity
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most battery and corded pole saws use an 8-inch bar, EGO's PS1000 uses a 10-inch bar, and gas power pruners like the ECHO run 12 inches. As with a chainsaw, a bar can cut wood up to roughly twice its length if you work from both sides, but on a pole saw you should plan on cutting limbs no thicker than the bar length in one pass.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice, most pruning cuts are 2 to 4 inches, well within the reach of an 8-inch bar. Pole saws struggle not with thickness but with control: a large limb is heavy, and when it starts to fall it can pinch the bar or swing toward you. That is why we recommend removing heavy limbs in sections, starting at the tip and working back toward the trunk.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chain matters too. Pole saws use narrow, low-kickback chains, often 3/8-inch low profile or 1/4-inch pitch, which cut smoothly with small motors. Keep the chain sharp and correctly tensioned: a dull chain makes you push, and pushing a saw 12 feet away is how cuts go wrong. Our guide to the <a href="/best-chainsaw-sharpener/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best chainsaw sharpeners</a> covers the files and grinders that fit these small chains.
          </p>

          <p>
            Oil the bar and chain every time. Every powered pole saw on our list has an automatic oiler, but you still have to fill the reservoir and check that oil is reaching the chain. Point the running saw at a light-colored surface for a few seconds; if you see a fine line of oil, the oiler is working.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Weight and Balance at Full Extension
          </h2>

          <p>
            A pole saw is a long lever, and every ounce at the cutting head feels like several by the time it is 10 feet away from your hands. That is why manufacturers compete on weight, and why carbon fiber shafts like the one on the EGO PS1000 are worth paying for if you prune often.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gas pole saws are the heaviest. The ECHO PPT-2620 weighs 17.4 pounds dry, with the engine at the bottom acting as a counterweight. Battery saws put the motor at the head and the battery near your hands, which shifts the balance forward. Manual saws are the lightest, around 7 pounds for the Silky Hayauchi, with the weight spread along the pole.
          </p>

          <p>
            A shoulder strap helps more than people expect, especially with heavier saws, because it lets your body carry the weight while your hands guide the saw. Several of the saws here have strap hooks. Take regular breaks: accuracy falls off noticeably once your shoulders tire, and a tired operator is when cuts drift.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you test a pole saw, extend it fully and hold it at a 60-degree angle for a minute. If that is a struggle, choose a lighter or shorter saw. You will make more careful cuts with a tool you can control than with one that reaches farther but wavers in your hands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-lines" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Power Lines and Pole Saw Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most important rule in pole sawing is simple: stay away from power lines. Keep yourself, the pole and any branch you are cutting at least 10 feet from any overhead line, which is the minimum clearance OSHA requires for unqualified workers near lines up to 50 kilovolts. Remember that a branch can swing toward a line as it falls.
          </p>

          <p>
            Aluminum poles conduct electricity, and fiberglass poles are not insulated unless they are specifically rated and tested for live-line work, which consumer pole saws are not. Wet wood, wet poles and damp gloves all make contact more dangerous. If any branch is touching or near a line, including the service line to your house, call your utility. Many will trim it for free.
          </p>

          <p>
            Never stand directly beneath the branch you are cutting. Position yourself to the side, at an angle, so a falling limb drops away from you. Wear a hard hat with a face shield or safety glasses, gloves and sturdy boots, and keep everyone else at least two tree lengths away from the drop zone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cut heavy limbs in stages. Make a small undercut first to prevent the bark from tearing down the trunk, then cut from the top a little further out. Remove the stub last. Never use a pole saw from a ladder, roof or tree. If a job requires climbing, it requires an arborist. Once limbs are on the ground, a <a href="/best-hookaroon-reviews/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">hookaroon</a> makes moving and stacking the cut wood far easier on your back.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pruning" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. When and How to Prune With a Pole Saw
          </h2>

          <p>
            Late winter, while trees are dormant, is the best time for most structural pruning. Without leaves, you can see the branch structure, cuts heal quickly when growth starts in spring, and pests and diseases are less active. Dead, damaged and crossing branches can be removed at any time of year.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cut just outside the branch collar, the slightly swollen ring where a branch meets the trunk. Cutting flush with the trunk removes the tissue that seals the wound, while leaving a long stub invites decay. On a pole saw, angle the bar so the cut follows the line of the collar.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not remove more than about a quarter of a tree's canopy in a single year, and avoid topping trees. Heavy pruning stresses the tree and triggers fast, weak regrowth. If a tree needs drastic work, spread it over several seasons or consult an arborist.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean up as you go. Cut limbs are trip hazards, and green wood stacked against a house invites insects. Small branches can be chipped or bundled for yard waste; larger rounds can be split for firewood. If you have a lot of wood to process, our guide to the <a href="/best-log-splitter/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best log splitters</a> covers the machines that make that job manageable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Maintenance and Storage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pole saws need the same basic care as chainsaws. Keep the chain sharp and correctly tensioned, keep the oil reservoir full, and clean sawdust and sap out of the bar groove and sprocket area after each use. A dirty bar groove starves the chain of oil and wears the bar quickly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check chain tension before every session and again after the first few cuts, because a new chain stretches. A correctly tensioned chain should snap back against the bar when you pull it out a little by hand, and still move freely along the bar with gloved fingers. Too loose and it can throw; too tight and it wears the bar and drains the battery.
          </p>

          <p>
            For telescoping poles, wipe the sections clean before collapsing them so grit does not score the inner tubes, and check that the locking collars or levers grip firmly. A pole that slips while you are mid-cut is both frustrating and dangerous.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store battery saws with the pack removed, indoors, at moderate temperatures. Store gas saws with the tank run dry or with stabilized fuel. Manual saws need nothing more than a wipe with a solvent to remove resin and the blade guard fitted. Hang long poles horizontally on wall hooks to keep them straight. If you also run a <a href="/best-hedge-trimmer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">hedge trimmer</a> on the same battery, a single charger and spare pack covers both.
          </p>
        </div>

