            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Working Near Power Lines</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Keep yourself, the pole and any branch at least 10 feet from overhead lines, and remember that falling limbs swing. Aluminum conducts and consumer fiberglass poles are not insulated. If a branch is near any line, including the service drop to your house, stop and call your utility instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Standing Under the Branch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The natural position for cutting overhead is directly beneath the limb, which is exactly where it lands. Stand to the side and cut at an angle so the branch falls away from you. Wear a hard hat and eye protection, and keep everyone well clear of the drop zone.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cutting Big Limbs in One Piece</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A heavy limb pinches the bar, tears bark down the trunk and can swing unpredictably as it falls toward you. Remove large limbs in sections from the tip back, use an undercut before the top cut, and take the final stub off cleanly outside the branch collar.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Pole Saw From a Ladder</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A pole saw exists so you do not need a ladder. Combining the two puts you off-balance with a running saw overhead and nothing to hold. If you cannot reach a branch from the ground, use a longer manual saw, leave it, or call an arborist.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Chain Tension and Oil</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A loose chain can derail and a dry one overheats and dulls quickly, and you cannot see either problem when the head is 12 feet up. Check tension and the oil reservoir before every session, and confirm the oiler is working before you lift the saw.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

