            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How high can a pole saw reach?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most battery and corded pole saws quote around 13 to 15 feet of total reach, which includes your own height and arm reach. Gas pruners with extensions can reach about 20 feet, and manual telescoping saws like the Silky Hayauchi extend to 21 feet. Plan to work a couple of feet below the quoted maximum for control.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How thick a branch can a pole saw cut?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plan on cutting limbs up to about the length of the bar in one pass: 8 inches for most battery saws, 10 to 12 inches for premium and gas models. In practice, most pruning cuts are 2 to 4 inches. Larger limbs should be removed in sections, and anything very large is a job for a tree service.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a manual pole saw better than a powered one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A manual saw is lighter, quieter, maintenance-free and often reaches higher, which makes it excellent for light pruning and tall trees. A powered saw is much faster and less tiring on limbs over a couple of inches. Many people with mature trees own one of each and choose by the job.
              </p>
